Actual root cause had nothing to do with the script (verified the script's language semantics work correctly in isolation): EditorApp::drawToolbar() pushed a style color only `if (playing_)`, then called the Play button, whose click handler flips `playing_` synchronously via setPlaying(), and then popped the style color `if (playing_)` again — now true post-click — producing an unbalanced ImGui::PopStyleColor() on every single Play click (Debug builds hit the ImGui assertion and abort(); Release builds silently corrupt the style-color stack). Reproduced live via the actual editor (attach script, click Play), confirmed the exact assertion (\u0022Calling PopStyleColor() too many times!\u0022, imgui.cpp:3356) by bisecting the script content down to an empty update() body — the crash was 100% present with zero script content, proving it was unrelated to the reported script.

Fix: capture `wasPlaying = playing_` before the button, and gate both the push and the pop on that captured snapshot instead of the live (mutable-mid-frame) `playing_` flag.

