{
    Camera headCam;
    Transform body;
    float look_speed = 3;
    func start()
    {
    }

    func update(float delta)
    {
        Vector2 input = Input.mouse.delta * look_speed * delta;
        print(input.str());
    }

    func physics_update(float delta)
    {
    }
}

Two symptoms observed, likely the same root cause: (1) the actor and ALL of its children disappear the moment Play starts; (2) the console/error log shows 'PlayerControl.update unknown identifier Input'. 'Input' (and Input.mouse.delta specifically) is supposed to be a recognized global -- this session's own notes describe Input.Mouse as 'a real nested member-access object, interpreted + native parity tested' (commit a779ba4), so this looks like a regression or a gap specific to this exact call shape (Input.mouse.delta, lowercase 'mouse', chained through look_speed*delta arithmetic, then Vector2.str()). Find the actual root cause -- do not just silence the error, and figure out why a script identifier error is making the whole actor hierarchy disappear rather than just failing that one line.

functions can be created freely with the func keyword a name and any  number of parameters. This then registeres that function for autocomplete, even in other scripts that reference the type the funciton is used in.

syntax is
func func_name(parameter_type param) : return_type
{
   code;
}

if no return type is specified you can still return whatever you want it just isnt required.

functions can be abstract and if they are they MUST be overriden in any inherited classes and cannot be called until they are,

overidden functions are called like this.
this.base.function_name();

you override a function by simply creating one with the same name but without the abstract keyword.

